      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "$\\,$ $x\\le 0\\text{ and }y$ is odd.\n\nThe negation of the composite predicate above is the following:\n\n$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for conjunctions states that\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of \"$x\\le 0\\text{ and }y$ is odd\" is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(x\\le 0\\text{ and }y\\text{ is odd}) & ≡¬(x\\le 0∧y\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & ≡¬(x\\le 0)∨¬(y\\text{ is odd}) \\\\  & ≡x>0∨y\\text{ is even} \\\\  & ≡x>0\\text{ or }y\\text{ is even}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
